{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And there was none who interpreted them, etc.</b> It says <b>otam</b> (\"them\") because there were two dreams. And although the verse uses singular language, ״אֶת חֲלוֹמוֹ״ (\"his dream\"), when it comes to inform that he found no interpreter, it was precise to say ״אוֹתָם״ (\"them\") — for both dreams together had no interpreter." "And its saying <b>lePharaoh</b> (\"for Pharaoh\") seemingly unnecessarily — it appears to say that the verse intends, in saying ״לְפַרְעֹה,״ that it is a well-known thing that the name Pharaoh is not a personal name but the name of the monarchy. Therefore you find that the chief cupbearer spoke to him ״פַּרְעֹה קָצַף״ (Genesis 40:2), and so on, and it is not proper etiquette to mention the king's name to his face in such a manner — rather, it is certainly the title of the kingship. And so our Sages of blessed memory said (Rashi on Psalms 34:1). From this it follows, it comes to give a reason why their interpretation was not accepted by him: because the dream was the king's dream, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 89) that the dreams of kings declare matters affecting the totality of the monarchy, and reason too dictates this, and they [the interpreters] were not offering an interpretation touching upon the monarchy but rather interpretations of ordinary people. And our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 89) said: ״לְפַרְעֹה״ — that their interpretation was not accepted for him [as fitting], and their words would have been understandable had it said ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר וְגוֹ׳ לוֹ״ (\"and there was none who interpreted… for him\")."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:29.864660784Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}